[
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "## Text with holes in it", "line": 1, "col": 1},
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "#", "line": 2, "col": 1},
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "# An `f` before the opening quote lets a string carry values: `{n}` writes `n`", "line": 3, "col": 1},
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "# as `to_str` would. Any expression may stand in a hole — a sum, a call, a", "line": 4, "col": 1},
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "# field, another `f\"…\"` — and the hole ends at the `}` that closes it, so a", "line": 5, "col": 1},
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "# map index or a nested literal inside it is read as it would be anywhere.", "line": 6, "col": 1},
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "# `{{` writes one brace. A literal without the `f` is exactly what it was.", "line": 7, "col": 1},
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "#", "line": 8, "col": 1},
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "# What a hole may hold is what `to_str` renders: an integer, a float, `bool`", "line": 9, "col": 1},
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "# or `str`. A record or an array has no text of its own, and a map index", "line": 10, "col": 1},
  {"kind": "comment", "text": "# answers a `V?` that must be made whole first — `check` says so, at the hole.", "line": 11, "col": 1},
